  "response": "Based on the architectural review of the proposed Agent Command Protocol (ACP) and Qwen Router → Kilo Execution Boundary (16.3 and 16.4), here is the verdict:\n\n### VERDICT\nThe proposed architecture provides a sound conceptual foundation for formalizing the AI-to-System interaction. The boundaries between agents (Qwen/Router, Kilo/Builder, OpenClaw/Orchestrator) are correctly delineated, and the proposal maintains the existing production boundaries (Render/Business Logic, Google Apps Script/Adapter).\n\nHowever, the protocol lacks sufficient structure to enforce the security and safety constraints required for autonomous task execution.\n\n### REQUIRED CHANGES\n1.  **ACP Authorization Structure (Authorization Scope)**: The `authorization` field (currently a single placeholder token) must be expanded to support **structured capabilities**. The ACP must explicitly include a machine-readable definition of the specific actions allowed for the requested `task` (e.g., `read_only`, `modify_files`, `run_tests`).\n2.  **ACP Constraint Definitions (Permitted Paths / Actions)**: The ACP requires a structured `allowed_scope` or `permitted_paths` field to constrain the Kilo Agent's operation to a specific subset of the repository or allowed tools, preventing unrestricted access even with a valid token.\n\n### RECOMMENDED CHANGES\n1.  **Execution Report Detail**: While the status reporting is adequate, the execution report should explicitly require a `command_audit_log_reference` or similar identifier to link the Kilo action to the originating ACP `request_id` for auditing purposes.\n\n### OPTIONAL CHANGES\n*   No optional changes identified.\n\n### NO CHANGE NEEDED\n*   The overall conceptual design of the ACP (16.3) and Qwen/Kilo boundary (16.4) as documentary/target architecture is correctly identified and does not falsely imply implementation.\n*   The distinction of roles (Router vs. Builder) and preservation of production boundaries (Render, Google Apps Script) is correctly handled.\n\n### SMALLEST NEXT STEP\n*   Update section 16.3 of `ARCHITECTURE.md` to define a structured `capabilities` object for the `authorization` field and an `allowed_scope` field for the `constraints` object, replacing the current \"placeholder-only\" descriptions. This must be done **only** after the structural design of these fields is reviewed and approved by the Director (Kyle).\n\n***\n\n*This review was performed under TEMPORARY CODEX FAILOVER MODE. No files were modified.*",
